# UI Components

Use shadcn/ui for anything shadcn already provides. Do not build custom
buttons, cards, inputs, dialogs, tables, etc. when a shadcn primitive covers
the need — install/use the shadcn component and wire up the implementation
around it instead.

Keep shadcn's default padding, spacing, and sizing. Do not override a
component's built-in structural classes (height, padding, gap, radius) unless
there's a concrete reason tied to this project's design system.

Colors are the one dimension expected to change — theme tokens (background,
foreground, primary, status colors, etc.) can be customized freely to match
this project's palette.

When editing a shadcn component's className, always merge with `cn(...)`
rather than replacing the base classes — never break the base
`buttonVariants`/similar variant classes a component ships with.

This is a strong default, not an absolute lock: if there's a good reason to
deviate (accessibility, a real UX problem, a case shadcn doesn't cover), it's
fine to suggest it — just flag that it's a deviation from stock shadcn rather
than silently reskinning components.

# UI Design & Visual Quality Skill

## Purpose

Build interfaces that feel intentionally designed, visually balanced, consistent, and production-ready.

Do not treat UI implementation as simply translating a description into HTML/CSS/components.

The agent must reason about:

* Layout
* Spacing
* Padding
* Visual hierarchy
* Typography
* Alignment
* Component proportions
* Density
* Grouping
* Responsive behavior
* Consistency
* Interaction states
* Visual polish

A technically functional interface is **not considered complete** if it looks cramped, awkward, excessively empty, misaligned, inconsistent, or visually unbalanced.

---

# 1. Core Principle

Before writing UI code, understand the visual hierarchy.

Every element belongs to a hierarchy:

```text
Page
 └── Section
      └── Content Group
           └── Component
                └── Element
```

Spacing must communicate this hierarchy.

Elements that belong together should have smaller spacing.

Groups that are conceptually separate should have larger spacing.

For example:

```text
Heading
8px
Description
16px
Actions

32px
Next section
```

Do not use the same spacing value everywhere.

Bad:

```text
Heading
24px
Description
24px
Button
24px
Next section
24px
```

Good spacing creates visual relationships.

---

# 2. Use a Consistent Spacing Scale

Do not invent arbitrary spacing values throughout the interface.

Prefer a spacing scale such as:

```text
4px
8px
12px
16px
20px
24px
32px
40px
48px
64px
80px
96px
```

Use the smallest appropriate value.

General guidance:

| Spacing | Typical purpose                                |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| 4px     | Very tight relationships                       |
| 8px     | Label → input, icon → text, heading → subtitle |
| 12px    | Related content within a component             |
| 16px    | Standard component spacing                     |
| 20px    | Comfortable component separation               |
| 24px    | Internal card padding / content groups         |
| 32px    | Sections / major groups                        |
| 40px    | Large component separation                     |
| 48px    | Major sections                                 |
| 64px    | Large page-level separation                    |
| 80–96px | Hero / major visual transitions                |

These are guidelines, not absolute rules.

The visual relationship matters more than blindly following numbers.

---

# 3. Page-Level Layout

Every page should establish a clear content container.

Consider:

* Maximum content width
* Horizontal page padding
* Vertical page padding
* Header-to-content spacing
* Section-to-section spacing
* Alignment of major elements

Avoid allowing content to sit directly against the viewport.

For desktop layouts, typical horizontal padding may be:

```text
24px → 32px → 40px → 48px
```

depending on the page.

On mobile, reduce this appropriately:

```text
16px → 20px → 24px
```

Do not make mobile layouts feel like compressed desktop layouts.

---

# 4. Container Alignment

Look for common alignment boundaries.

Related content should generally share the same left and right edges.

For example:

```text
|--------------------------------------|
| Page heading                         |
| Description                          |
|--------------------------------------|
| Card                                 |
|                                      |
|--------------------------------------|
```

Avoid situations where:

* The heading starts at 32px
* The card starts at 48px
* The table starts at 24px
* The buttons start at 40px

unless there is an intentional design reason.

Alignment creates visual order.

---

# 5. Section Spacing

Different sections should have obvious separation.

Use larger spacing between sections than inside sections.

Example:

```text
Page title
8px
Page description
32px
First content section
48px
Second content section
48px
Third content section
```

Do not let unrelated sections visually merge together.

Conversely, do not put enormous gaps between related sections simply because whitespace "looks modern."

Whitespace must communicate structure.

---

# 6. Component Padding

Components should have enough internal breathing room.

For cards, panels, modals, dropdowns, forms, etc., consider:

```text
16px
20px
24px
32px
```

depending on component size and density.

A component should not feel cramped.

Bad:

```text
┌─────────────────────────┐
│Title                    │
│Description              │
│Button                   │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

when everything is touching the edges.

Better:

```text
┌─────────────────────────┐
│                         │
│  Title                  │
│  Description            │
│                         │
│  Button                 │
│                         │
└─────────────────────────┘
```

But do not automatically maximize padding.

The correct padding depends on:

* Component size
* Content density
* Importance
* Screen size
* Surrounding whitespace

---

# 7. Nested Spacing

When components contain nested elements, spacing should become progressively tighter.

Example:

```text
Card
  24px padding

  Heading
    8px
  Description
    16px
  Form fields
    12–16px
  Actions
    24px
```

Think:

```text
Page spacing > Section spacing > Component spacing > Element spacing
```

This is one of the most important rules.

---

# 8. Typography Spacing

Typography needs its own hierarchy.

Typical relationships:

```text
Heading
8px
Subtitle / description

16–24px
Content
```

Avoid excessive spacing between text that belongs together.

For example, this is usually wrong:

```text
Heading

32px

Description
```

unless intentionally designed that way.

Similarly, don't cram:

```text
Heading
2px
Description
```

Text should feel connected but breathable.

---

# 9. Forms

Forms must have clear vertical rhythm.

Typical structure:

```text
Field label
8px
Input
16px
Next field
```

For related fields:

```text
First name     Last name
              

Email
```

The gap between fields should generally be smaller than the gap between the form and another unrelated section.

Group related fields together.

Use larger spacing when starting a new logical group.

Example:

```text
Personal Information

Name
16px
Email
16px
Phone

32px

Address

Address line
16px
City
16px
Country
```

---

# 10. Buttons and Controls

Buttons should have appropriate internal padding.

Consider:

* Height
* Horizontal padding
* Icon-to-label spacing
* Button-to-button spacing
* Alignment with nearby controls

Avoid:

```text
[Save] [Cancel]
```

with buttons touching each other.

Use a deliberate gap.

Usually:

```text
8px
12px
16px
```

depending on context.

Do not make every button unnecessarily large.

---

# 11. Cards

Cards should have internal hierarchy.

A typical card:

```text
24px padding

Eyebrow / label
8px
Title
8px
Description

24px
Main content

24px
Actions
```

But this should change according to the content.

Do not apply identical card padding to every card regardless of size.

A dense data card may need less.

A marketing or informational card may need more.

---

# 12. Tables and Data-Dense Interfaces

Do not use the same spacing philosophy for dashboards and marketing pages.

Data-heavy interfaces should prioritize:

* Scanability
* Density
* Alignment
* Clear row separation
* Consistent column spacing

Avoid enormous whitespace in tables.

Rows should be comfortable but efficient.

For example:

```text
32–48px row height
```

may be appropriate depending on the interface.

Use whitespace to separate groups, not to make every row feel oversized.

---

# 13. Dashboards

For dashboards, establish a clear grid.

Consider:

```text
Page padding
↓
Page heading
↓
Summary / filters
↓
Primary content
↓
Secondary content
```

Cards should align to the same grid.

Avoid random card widths.

Avoid:

```text
Card A: 387px
Card B: 421px
Card C: 356px
```

unless the design intentionally calls for it.

Prefer a coherent grid system.

---

# 14. Responsive Design

Spacing must adapt to screen size.

Do not simply shrink everything.

Desktop:

```text
Page padding: 40–48px
Section spacing: 48–64px
Card padding: 24–32px
```

Mobile:

```text
Page padding: 16–24px
Section spacing: 32–48px
Card padding: 16–24px
```

These are examples, not hard requirements.

Always consider the actual content.

A layout that looks spacious on desktop may become cramped on mobile.

A layout that looks elegant on desktop may become excessively tall on mobile.

---

# 15. Visual Hierarchy

Before adding or changing spacing, ask:

1. What is the most important thing on this screen?
2. What belongs together?
3. What should feel separate?
4. Where should the user's eye go first?
5. What information is secondary?
6. Which elements need breathing room?
7. Which elements should be visually compact?

Spacing should reinforce these answers.

Do not use spacing randomly.

---

# 16. Don't Overuse Whitespace

Whitespace is not automatically good design.

Too little:

```text
CRAMPED
```

Too much:

```text
        Heading


        Description



        Button
```

The goal is **intentional whitespace**.

Ask:

> Is this space helping establish hierarchy, grouping, or emphasis?

If not, reduce it.

---

# 17. Don't Overuse Padding

A common AI mistake is fixing poor layouts by adding padding.

Do not automatically respond to a cramped interface with:

```css
padding: 32px;
```

First determine the cause.

The problem might actually be:

* Incorrect line height
* Incorrect component width
* Incorrect gap
* Missing grouping
* Wrong font size
* Incorrect grid
* Poor alignment
* Excessive content
* Wrong container width

Fix the underlying problem.

---

# 18. Use Gap Where Appropriate

Prefer layout mechanisms that express relationships clearly.

For flex/grid layouts:

```css
gap
```

is often preferable to arbitrary margins between siblings.

Example:

```css
display: flex;
gap: 16px;
```

rather than manually applying different margins to each child.

However, do not blindly replace all margins with `gap`.

Use whichever expresses the design relationship most clearly.

---

# 19. Avoid Magic Numbers

Do not produce a UI containing:

```text
17px
23px
27px
31px
37px
43px
```

unless there is a specific reason.

Prefer a coherent design scale.

Consistency is more important than finding the theoretically perfect pixel value.

---

# 20. Component Consistency

If the application has multiple:

* Cards
* Buttons
* Inputs
* Modals
* Headers
* Sidebars
* Tables
* Tabs
* Dropdowns

they should feel like they belong to the same design system.

Before creating a new pattern, inspect existing components.

Reuse established:

* Padding
* Border radius
* Typography
* Heights
* Gaps
* Shadows
* Colors
* Interaction states

Do not create a new spacing convention for every screen.

---

# 21. Visual Density

Every interface has a density level.

Consider whether the product is:

* Dense
* Comfortable
* Spacious

Examples:

### Dense

Good for:

* Admin panels
* Tables
* Financial data
* Operations dashboards

### Comfortable

Good for:

* HR systems
* Productivity apps
* General business software

### Spacious

Good for:

* Marketing
* Landing pages
* Editorial interfaces
* High-emphasis onboarding

Do not make an administrative dashboard look like a marketing website.

---

# 22. Empty States

Empty states should not simply be:

```text
No data
```

Use intentional composition:

```text
Icon / illustration

Heading

Short explanation

Primary action
```

Spacing should make this feel like a designed state rather than an error message.

---

# 23. Loading and Error States

Loading, error, success, and empty states must follow the same spacing system as the normal interface.

Do not allow temporary states to break the visual layout.

---

# 24. Interaction States

Every interactive element should consider:

* Default
* Hover
* Focus
* Active
* Disabled
* Loading
* Error
* Success

Spacing should remain stable when states change.

Avoid layouts where text wrapping or padding changes dramatically between states.

---

# 25. Icons

Icons should be visually aligned with their surrounding text.

Consider:

* Size
* Stroke weight
* Icon-to-text gap
* Vertical alignment
* Button padding

Typical icon/text spacing:

```text
6px
8px
```

Do not allow icons to visually float away from their labels.

---

# 26. Borders, Radius and Spacing Work Together

Do not treat spacing independently from component styling.

A component with:

* large border radius
* heavy shadow
* large padding
* huge typography

can quickly become visually bloated.

A component with:

* small radius
* tight padding
* small typography

can feel dense.

Evaluate the complete composition.

---

# 27. Before Coding

Before implementing a substantial UI, mentally or explicitly identify:

```text
Page container
Page hierarchy
Major sections
Section spacing
Component grid
Component padding
Typography hierarchy
Control spacing
Responsive behavior
```

Do not immediately start writing components.

Understand the layout first.

---

# 28. After Coding: Mandatory Visual QA

**This step is mandatory.**

After implementing a UI, inspect the actual rendered result.

Do not assume that correct CSS means correct design.

Perform a dedicated visual QA pass.

Check:

### Spacing

* Is page padding consistent?
* Are sections sufficiently separated?
* Are related elements close enough?
* Are unrelated elements separated enough?
* Are cards internally padded correctly?
* Is anything touching the edges unnecessarily?
* Is there excessive whitespace anywhere?

### Alignment

* Do major elements share alignment boundaries?
* Are cards aligned?
* Are headings aligned with their content?
* Are buttons aligned?
* Are form fields aligned?

### Hierarchy

* Is the primary content obvious?
* Are secondary elements appropriately subdued?
* Does spacing communicate grouping?
* Are sections visually distinguishable?

### Density

* Does the interface feel cramped?
* Does it feel unnecessarily empty?
* Does the amount of information shown feel appropriate?

### Typography

* Are headings too large?
* Are body texts too small?
* Are line heights appropriate?
* Are text blocks too wide?
* Are labels and descriptions properly grouped?

### Responsive behavior

Check at least:

```text
Mobile
Tablet
Desktop
Large desktop
```

Look specifically for:

* Excessive horizontal padding
* Content touching edges
* Broken grids
* Awkward wrapping
* Excessive vertical height
* Inconsistent spacing

---

# 29. Perform a Dedicated "Spacing Pass"

Before considering the work complete, perform a second pass whose only purpose is spacing and composition.

Do not focus on functionality during this pass.

Ask:

> If I removed all borders, shadows, and colors, would the layout still look well structured based purely on spacing and alignment?

If the answer is no, fix the layout.

Specifically inspect:

```text
Page → Section
Section → Component
Component → Group
Group → Element
Element → Element
```

Each level should have an intentional spacing relationship.

---

# 30. Visual QA Priority Order

When something looks wrong, fix problems in this order:

```text
1. Overall layout
2. Container width
3. Alignment
4. Spacing / padding
5. Typography
6. Component sizing
7. Visual hierarchy
8. Borders / radius / shadows
9. Colors
10. Micro-details
```

Do not polish shadows while the layout is poorly spaced.

Do not adjust colors to compensate for bad hierarchy.

---

# 31. Preserve Existing Design Language

When modifying an existing application, do not redesign everything unnecessarily.

First understand the existing design language.

Identify:

* Existing spacing scale
* Existing typography
* Existing component dimensions
* Existing border radius
* Existing colors
* Existing grid
* Existing responsive behavior

Then extend the system.

Consistency with the existing product is generally more important than introducing a new aesthetic.

---

# 32. Avoid "AI UI"

Do not produce interfaces that look obviously generated.

Avoid:

* Excessive rounded cards
* Excessive gradients
* Random glassmorphism
* Huge headings everywhere
* Excessive shadows
* Arbitrary whitespace
* Every element inside a card
* Repeated identical cards
* Generic dashboard layouts
* Random icons
* Inconsistent spacing
* Overly decorative UI without purpose

The interface should feel like a deliberate product designed by someone who understands the user's workflow.

---

# 33. Implementation Rule

When asked to create or modify UI:

### Step 1

Understand the existing design system.

### Step 2

Understand the content hierarchy.

### Step 3

Establish the page/container layout.

### Step 4

Establish section spacing.

### Step 5

Establish component dimensions and padding.

### Step 6

Establish typography hierarchy.

### Step 7

Implement responsive behavior.

### Step 8

Render/inspect the result.

### Step 9

Perform a dedicated spacing and alignment pass.

### Step 10

Perform a final visual polish pass.

Only then consider the UI complete.

---

# 34. Definition of Done

A UI is not finished simply because:

* It compiles
* It works
* The API works
* The buttons work
* The routes work
* The components render

It is finished when it is also:

* Visually balanced
* Consistently spaced
* Properly aligned
* Responsive
* Hierarchically clear
* Appropriately dense
* Consistent with the product's design language
* Comfortable to use
* Free from obvious visual awkwardness

**Functionality and visual quality are equally part of completion.**

---

# Final Instruction

Whenever you build UI, **actively reason about spacing rather than adding padding reactively.**

Do not ask:

> "Where can I add padding?"

Ask:

> "What elements belong together, what elements are separate, and what spacing should communicate that relationship?"

Then implement the spacing hierarchy and verify it against the rendered interface.

**Never skip the visual QA and spacing pass.**
